<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nadine Gordimer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1923–2014</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nadine Gordimer was a South African novelist and short-story writer whose work, including The Conservationist and Burger's Daughter, examined the moral and political life of her country under apartheid. Several of her books were banned by the government. She was a member of the ANC and received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nadine-gordimer.jpg" alt="Nadine Gordimer" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boberger. Photo: Bengt Oberger · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nadine_Gordimer_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
